WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.600
A resting adult moves roughly six litres of air and five litres of blood through the

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:09.620
lungs every minute.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.780
Do those two numbers tell you what the blood leaves with?

4
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:18.280
They do not. What settles it is whether air and blood arrive at the same places in

5
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.280
the same proportion.

6
00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:25.243
For a given inspired gas and mixed venous blood, one quantity mainly sets a unit's

7
00:00:25.243 --> 00:00:29.263
gas. That quantity is its ventilation divided by its perfusion.

8
00:00:29.903 --> 00:00:34.683
At a ratio of zero, a unit is perfused and not ventilated, which makes it a shunt.

9
00:00:35.343 --> 00:00:38.203
Its blood leaves with the oxygen content it arrived with.

10
00:00:38.643 --> 00:00:43.443
At an infinite ratio, a unit is ventilated and not perfused, which makes it dead

11
00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:44.443
space.

12
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:48.913
Real lungs hold a spread of ratios, which an inert-gas method recovers.

13
00:00:49.753 --> 00:00:55.013
In four young semirecumbent normal subjects, 95.5 percent of both blood flow and

14
00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:58.513
ventilation lay between ratios of 0.3 and 2.1.

15
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:00.213
There was no shunt on air.

16
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:04.713
Reference values for healthy people put the mean of the blood-flow distribution at

17
00:01:04.713 --> 00:01:10.053
0.80 to 1.00. Textbooks quote a single whole-lung ratio of 0.8.

18
00:01:10.053 --> 00:01:13.033
That is not the same as the mean of a measured distribution.

19
00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:17.253
A spread lowers arterial PO2 by itself.

20
00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:21.613
That holds even when every unit equilibrates with its own alveolar gas.

21
00:01:22.313 --> 00:01:24.053
The two averages are taken differently.

22
00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:29.693
Expired alveolar gas is weighted by ventilation, so well-ventilated units dominate

23
00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:35.073
it. Arterial blood is weighted by blood flow, so poorly ventilated units count for

24
00:01:35.073 --> 00:01:36.073
more in it.

25
00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:41.417
Blood from different units then mixes by oxygen content, not by partial pressure.

26
00:01:42.377 --> 00:01:45.417
Content is how much oxygen each decilitre of blood carries.

27
00:01:46.177 --> 00:01:51.097
The oxygen dissociation curve, which plots content against partial pressure, is flat

28
00:01:51.097 --> 00:01:55.317
at the top. So a high-ratio unit adds almost nothing per decilitre.

29
00:01:55.837 --> 00:01:57.997
A low-ratio unit subtracts a great deal.

30
00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:02.497
Alveolar PO2 is calculated, not measured.

31
00:02:03.157 --> 00:02:07.437
The result is an alveolar-arterial oxygen difference, even when every unit

32
00:02:07.437 --> 00:02:09.577
equilibrates with its own alveolar gas.

33
00:02:10.257 --> 00:02:12.517
It is a difference, not a diffusion gradient.

34
00:02:13.237 --> 00:02:18.317
Each unit's ratio mainly sets its gas, for a given inspired gas and mixed venous

35
00:02:18.317 --> 00:02:22.837
blood. A spread of ratios alone can widen the alveolar-arterial difference.
